This project is an original work that was designed after studying two
open-source DeepSeek Harness (DSH) plugins. Their authors and projects are
credited below. No source code was copied verbatim; the mechanism and the
DSH plugin contracts were re-implemented for a different purpose.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) AIQuit「爷不干了」 — https://github.com/Jia-HuaWu/AIQuit
   Author: Jia-HuaWu (https://github.com/Jia-HuaWu)
   License: MIT
   Influence: the "programmatic adjudication" pattern — intercepting each user
   turn in the `agent/pre-step` waterfall, running a tiny history-free side
   request to make a decision, and refusing the turn by writing a fully
   contract-compliant step lifecycle while the main model is never invoked.
   The session-event contract constraints (turn/step, message.content, and the
   mandatory `stream` array) in AiQuestion follow the hard-won lessons
   documented by AIQuit, and its draggable gear + settings panel layout is the
   direct model for the AiQuestion settings widget.
   AIQuit is the inverse counterpart of this project: it refuses work, while
   AiQuestion clarifies intent before work begins.

2) dsh-ops — https://github.com/T-Auto/dsh-ops
   Author: T-Auto (https://github.com/T-Auto)
   License: MIT AND Apache-2.0 (plugin source MIT; vendored FastCtx Apache-2.0)
   Influence: the token-efficiency philosophy and DSH plugin packaging —
   component toggles, a zero fixed-cost prompt baseline, aggressive use of
   cheap side requests, and the principle of "real" token savings (avoid
   rework rather than merely shorten output). AiQuestion adopts that spirit for
   intent clarification and mirrors the manifest/bundle layout
   (package.json `dsh.bundle.patch` + cordis.patch.yml) shown by dsh-ops.

Both upstream projects are also DSH plugins, and AiQuestion is an independent,
original project. If you redistribute AiQuestion, keep this NOTICE so the
original authors remain credited.
